# Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)

Decisions must not live only in chat history or meeting notes. Whenever a qualifying decision is reached, **proactively offer to write an ADR**. Ask once; draft end-to-end if the user agrees.

## When to offer an ADR

Offer when **all three** criteria hold:

1. **Reusable** — will guide future similar choices, not a one-off project setting or environment-specific configuration.
2. **Architecturally significant** — touches a platform, integration pattern, identity/security baseline, data ownership model, canonical entity definition, vendor or product selection, application portfolio decision, or a cross-cutting principle.
3. **Trade-offs were considered** — at least two real options were weighed; there was a decision to make, not just an implementation to do.

**Skip ADRs for:** implementation details, tunable environment settings, low-stakes preferences, single-project tactical choices, and decisions that are trivially reversible.

## Domain scope

ADRs apply across all four TOGAF domains — not just Technology.

| Domain | When an ADR is warranted | Default home |
|--------|-------------------------|--------------|
| **Technology (Phase D)** | Platform choice, integration technology, identity/security baseline, hosting model, runtime selection | `TechnologyArchitecture/` |
| **Application (Phase C)** | Portfolio rationalisation decision (Tolerate / Invest / Eliminate), application consolidation approach, build vs buy for a capability | `ApplicationArchitecture/` |
| **Data (Phase C)** | System of record assignment, canonical data model adoption, master data management approach, data ownership across systems | `DataArchitecture/` |
| **Business (Phase B)** | Capability ownership, operating model decision, consolidation principle (e.g. "one system of record per capability"), sourcing model for a business function | `BusinessArchitecture/` |

Paths are derived from `AGENTS.md`. If a domain-specific ADR directory is not defined there, default to the domain folder root.

## Format

**Filename:** `<DOMAIN_DIR>/ADR-NNNN-<kebab-slug>.md`
Use the next free `NNNN` across all domains — the ADR counter is global, not per-domain.

**Sections, in order:**

```markdown
# ADR-NNNN — <one-line statement of the decision>

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Status** | Proposed (Draft) |
| **Date** | YYYY-MM-DD |
| **Owner** | <role or name> |
| **Domain** | <Business | Application | Data | Technology> |
| **ADM phase** | <B | C-App | C-Data | D> |
| **Scope** | <what this decision applies to — system, programme, platform> |
| **Related** | <links to related ADRs, documents, principles> |
| **Principles** | <P-NN (Name); P-NN (Name) — or "none" if genuinely none apply> |

## Context

<Why this decision is being made now. Working assumptions. Business driver or stakeholder concern that triggered it. What changes if this decision is not made.>

## Options considered

| Option | Effort | Change impact | Strategic upside | Notes |
|--------|--------|---------------|-----------------|-------|
| A — <name> | | | | |
| B — <name> | | | | |
| *(discarded)* — <name> | — | — | — | <why discarded> |

## Decision

**Chosen:** <Option X — one sentence stating the decision.>

**Triggers for alternatives:** <conditions under which Option Y or Z would be preferred instead>

## Decision criteria

1. <Most important criterion — state it as a testable condition>
2. <Second criterion>
3. <…>

## Consequences

**Positive:** <what this enables or improves>

**Accepted trade-offs:** <what is worse or harder as a result — be honest>

## Operational guardrails

<What must be true operationally for this decision to hold — SLA, monitoring, rotation, cost threshold, review trigger. Omit section if not applicable.>

## Open items

- <anything unresolved that would change the decision if answered differently>
```

## Behavioural rules

- **Ask once:** when a chat is heading toward a qualifying decision, offer *"Capture this as ADR-NNNN?"* — do not repeat the offer.
- **Draft end-to-end:** if the user says yes, write the complete ADR, not a stub. Populate every section; use *TBD* only for genuinely unknown information and put it under **Open items**.
- **Populate Principles:** always check `principles.md` (path from `AGENTS.md`) for relevant P-NN IDs. If the file doesn't exist, note it as an open item. If the decision conflicts with a principle, name the conflict in **Context** or **Consequences** — do not silently omit it.
- **Supersession:** if a decision supersedes an earlier ADR, set the old ADR's Status to `Superseded by ADR-MMMM (YYYY-MM-DD)` and add a `Related` link in both directions.
- **Don't invent decisions:** if the user has not actually made a choice, list the ambiguity under **Open items**, not under **Decision**.
- **Existing ADR edits:** when editing an existing ADR, preserve section order. Never silently change **Decision** or **Status** — surface the change to the user first.

## Domain-specific triggers (quick reference)

**Technology ADR — offer when:**
- A new platform, cloud service, or runtime is being adopted
- An integration pattern (API, event, batch, data virtualisation) is being standardised
- An identity, security, or networking baseline is being established or changed
- A vendor or product is being selected for a platform-level capability

**Application ADR — offer when:**
- An application is being classified as Invest / Migrate / Eliminate in a portfolio review
- A build vs buy vs SaaS decision is being made for a business capability
- An application consolidation target is being selected

**Data ADR — offer when:**
- A system of record is being assigned or changed for a canonical entity
- A master data management approach is being chosen (hub / registry / coexistence)
- A canonical data model is being adopted or a significant entity is being redefined
- Data ownership is being reassigned across systems

**Business ADR — offer when:**
- A capability is being assigned to a single business owner
- An operating model decision is being made that affects multiple teams
- A consolidation principle is being established (e.g. "one contract management system")
- A sourcing model for a business function is being decided (insource / outsource / shared service)
